WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.750
- [Narrator] Did you buy
or sell crypto this year

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.490
or maybe use it to purchase something?

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.230
Even as small as a sandwich?

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.210
If so that transaction might need

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.220
to be counted on your taxes.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.260
- It's especially important
to talk about taxes

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.630
during this 2022 tax filing season,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.620
because so many people
bought or sold crypto

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.460
during 2021 for the first time.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.490
- [Narrator] The number
of crypto owners worldwide

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.360
nearly tripled in 2021,

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.130
growing from 106 million in January

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.600
to 295 million in December

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.900
according to a report by crypto.com.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.260
For those crypto newcomers,

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.990
Wall Street Journal tax columnist

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.900
Laura Saunders has a warning.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.450
- I think that the IRS has
a special focus on crypto,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.610
because they're uncomfortable with it.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.530
They see it as a kind of a
threat to the tax system.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:50.020
They think it may attract
criminal activity,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.130
money laundering, or tax evasion.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.630
So the IRS is paying very special

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.300
close attention to cryptocurrency.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.880
- [Narrator] Here are five
things you need to know

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.943
about taxes for cryptocurrencies.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.780
First, how to report.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.640
You'll see this question

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.140
on the Form 1040 right below your name.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.990
Be sure you answer it accurately.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.890
- If you don't answer the
question or if you lie,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.390
and they later have a case
against you involving crypto,

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.780
it will probably be a lot easier

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.930
for the IRS to win that case.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:21.530
- [Narrator] But once you check the box,

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.470
how do you know how much you owe?

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.793
To find out, let's look at how
the IRS defines the currency.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.504
- Since 2014, the Internal Revenue Service

00:01:30.504 --> 00:01:34.840
has classified
cryptocurrencies as property,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.780
usually investment property.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.100
That means they're not
like dollars or euros.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.130
They're much more like shares of stock

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.460
like Apple or IBM shares.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.560
- [Narrator] And like
income on stocks and bonds,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.530
the law has different rules for short

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.650
and long term gains on crypto.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.450
- If you've held it for a year or less,

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.970
it's a short term capital gain or loss.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.350
If you've held it for longer than a year,

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.920
it's a long term capital gain or loss.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.580
And those gains, if
they're long-term gains,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.090
can be taxed at much lower
rates than say wages.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.010
So the top rate on wages
might be about 40%.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.130
The top rate on long term capital gain

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.000
on cryptocurrency would be 20%.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.530
That's a big difference.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.270
- [Narrator] The good news
is that capital losses

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.200
can be used to offset the gains.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.870
Meaning you can pay less in taxes

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.340
for the money you gained in crypto

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.053
if you also lost money
investing in the currency.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.120
But capital gains on an investment

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.350
isn't the only way people use crypto.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.400
Some are using the currency to buy goods

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.370
like that sandwich or even a yacht.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.866
- If you're holding your
crypto in a taxable account

00:02:39.866 --> 00:02:43.350
versus say a retirement
account like an IRA,

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.300
and you use it to purchase something,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.580
then you have a taxable transaction.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.320
- [Narrator] This isn't a new idea.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.160
- That's just as though
you were buying that item

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.430
with shares of stock, say
shares of Apple stock.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.530
And just the way that if
you transferred the stock

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:02.170
to someone, you'd have to tell the IRS

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.830
and have a loss or gain,

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.500
the same thing is true with crypto.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.050
- [Narrator] Also similar to
stocks, the IRS is trading

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.760
between two different entities,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.130
such as from Apple stock to
Microsoft or in crypto's case,

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:14.630
from Bitcoin to Ethereum

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.550
are taxable transfers.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.120
What we just discussed
applies to buying and selling

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.660
cryptocurrency and other tangible goods.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.810
Where it gets trickier is if
someone is paid for services

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.960
in crypto, which happened
on a big stage in 2021.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.250
The Mayors of both New York City and Miami

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.870
tweeted their plans to
accept at least one paycheck

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.320
in Bitcoin, plus more businesses
are offering pay in crypto

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.280
to attract younger workers.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.180
- In that case it's compensation.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:45.500
So you owe income tax on it,
but you also owe payroll taxes

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.650
such as Medicare taxes
and Social Security taxes.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.750
After you pay those taxes,
it becomes an investment.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.100
- [Narrator] But if the crypto is a gift

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.060
rather than a payment for a service...

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.470
- Neither the giver nor the recipient

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.510
owes any income tax on that transfer.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.120
But when the recipient
ultimately sells the crypto,

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.570
they have to use the giver's cost basis

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.630
to figure the capital gain or loss.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.470
It's not the value at the
time the gift was received.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.370
It was the value when the giver bought it.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.820
- [Narrator] And if that value goes up,

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.240
the recipient would have
to pay taxes on the gain

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.030
when they go to trade or sell.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.430
So keeping a clear record is key.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.080
Many people have what they
call hot and cold wallets

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.902
for security purposes, but this
also helps organize records.

00:04:32.902 --> 00:04:34.821
- The cold wallet would be kind of

00:04:34.821 --> 00:04:36.124
like your savings account.

00:04:36.124 --> 00:04:36.980
You don't touch it.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.560
The hot wallet would be the
one you're trading from.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.067
And so you could tell the IRS,

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.306
"No, you don't have to
worry about the cold wallet.

00:04:43.306 --> 00:04:44.300
Let's look at the hot wallet.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.580
This is the one I was working with."

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.690
- [Narrator] Transferring
crypto between these two wallets

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.200
is a non-taxable transaction.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.960
The IRS's rules on
cryptocurrency are evolving

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.010
as crypto itself evolves and
moves into the mainstream.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.010
Some digital assets like NFTs,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.700
don't have official guidance yet,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.970
but many tax specialists
think they should be taxed

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.670
like collectibles, such
as art or antiques.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.780
In that case, the top tax rate is 28%,

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.080
which is higher than the top
rate on cryptocurrency trades.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.440
- My advice would be even
if there aren't rules,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.920
don't think you can do
whatever you want to do.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.640
If you don't report correctly,

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.390
the IRS may find out about it.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:24.033
For instance, they've talked to judges

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:26.980
and been successful in
getting customer records

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.540
from places like Coinbase
that they can use

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.480
to match against people's tax
returns and generate cases.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.760
- [Narrator] The tax deadline
for 2022 is April 18th.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.090
For further tax questions,

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.092
see "The Wall Street
Journal's 2022 Tax Guide."

00:05:41.092 --> 00:05:43.759
(gentle music)

